/// A format that values are written in and read from, such as JSON, CSV or a database row.
/// This module declares the stdlib's formats, so a type can name the formats it may be
/// written in without importing their modules. Each format's module declares the attribute
/// that opts types into it.
///
/// A package adds a format the same way: a class that implements `Codec`, and an attribute
/// that implements `Codable` of it.
///
/// ```
/// pub class YamlCodec implements Codec {}
///
/// pub attribute yaml implements Codable<YamlCodec> {}
/// ```
pub interface Codec {}

/// The JSON format, for `Codable<JsonCodec>` in signatures that send or read JSON.
pub class JsonCodec implements Codec {}

/// The CSV format.
pub class CsvCodec implements Codec {}

/// The form-encoding format (`application/x-www-form-urlencoded`), as browsers submit forms
/// and query strings carry them.
pub class FormCodec implements Codec {}

/// The database row format: how a record's fields map to columns, and back.
pub class DbCodec implements Codec {}

/// What a session can hold: small values, not records. Numbers, `Bool`, `String`, backed
/// enums, distinct types of those, and stdlib types that say so, such as `TokenHash`. A
/// session is for remembering who someone is and where they are in a flow; keep anything
/// bigger in the database, and put its ID in the session.
pub class SessionCodec implements Codec {}

/// A type that can be written with the codec `C` and read back. A type implements it by
/// carrying that codec's attribute: `@json` makes a type `Codable<JsonCodec>`, and the
/// compiler generates the code that walks its fields, each of which must be codable too.
/// Built-in scalars, backed enums, and `List`, `Set`, `Map` and `T?` of codable types are
/// codable with every codec. So is a type that implements `Codable<Codec>`, as the
/// stdlib's value types (`Instant`, `Url`) do. A distinct type is written as its base; one
/// with a `private` wrap must implement `Parseable`, which reads it back.
///
/// Opting in is per codec, so a database record that holds a password hash isn't
/// `Codable<JsonCodec>` and can't be sent as JSON by mistake.
///
/// ```
/// fn report<T: Codable<JsonCodec>>(summary: T) -> Response {
/// 	return Response.json(summary, status: Status.ACCEPTED)
/// }
/// ```
pub interface Codable<C: Codec> {}

/// A way of writing a name made of several words. Codecs use it to map field names to keys
/// or columns, so a type states its naming policy in one place.
///
/// ```
/// @db(table: "users", columns: Case.Snake)
/// record User {
/// 	createdAt: Instant
/// }
/// ```
pub enum Case {
	/// Lower case, words joined with `_`: `created_at`.
	Snake
	/// First word lower case, later words capitalised: `createdAt`.
	Camel
	/// Every word capitalised: `CreatedAt`.
	Pascal
	/// Lower case, words joined with `-`: `created-at`.
	Kebab
}
